# November 20, 2026: Albert County and the Dorchester-Sackville end of Westmorland County, New Brunswick: the shiretown at
# Hopewell Cape, the albertite and gypsum country round Hillsborough, Riverside-Albert, Cape Enrage and the Fundy National
# Park headquarters at Alma; then the stone houses and the penitentiary of Dorchester, and Sackville's carriage factory,
# station and oldest houses. Region nb was drawn by tools/choose.py --claim --state qc,nb,ns,pe,nl.
# Sources opened in this session: the Canadian Register of Historic Places statement for every place but two (historicplaces.ca,
# read with tools/page.py --all; the character-defining elements say what survives and what it is built of); Wikipedia as raw
# text (tools/wiki_raw.py) for the Albert County Museum, Cape Enrage Lighthouse, Albert Mines, Dorchester Penitentiary and the
# New Brunswick Railway Museum; Lighthouse Friends for Cape Enrage; CBC News for Albert Mines (access) and the Sackville
# Harness shop (closing); and the operators' own pages for hours: albertcountymuseum.com, steeveshousemuseum.ca,
# keillorhousemuseum.com (Westmorland Historical Society), tantramarheritage.ca (Tantramar Heritage Trust) and Parks Canada's
# Fundy pages. Addresses were placed with the ArcGIS geocoder (PointAddress results).
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
def L(t,u): return [t,u]
def HP(t,i): return ['Canadian Register of Historic Places: '+t,'https://www.historicplaces.ca/en/rep-reg/place-lieu.aspx?id=%d'%i]
FUNDYH=L('Parks Canada: Fundy National Park hours','https://parks.canada.ca/pn-np/nb/fundy/visit/heures-hours')
KEIL=L('Westmorland Historical Society: hours and admission','https://keillorhousemuseum.com/hours/')
THT=L('Tantramar Heritage Trust','https://tantramarheritage.ca/')
BATCH={'key':'nb_albert_acoa','label':'Albert County, Dorchester and Sackville, New Brunswick','added':'2026-11-20 05:12'}
PLACES=[
# ---- Albert County: Hopewell Cape and Hillsborough ----
N('albert-county-museum-hopewell-cape','Albert County Museum (old shiretown: gaol, courthouse and county offices)','Hopewell Cape','nb','civic','standing',45.84902,-64.57710,'site','open','walk','','1845–1904',
  'Hopewell Cape was the shiretown, the county seat, of Albert County, and its county buildings survive together on one six-acre site. The tax office, the records office and the county gaol date from the creation of the county in 1845; the courthouse was built in 1904 to replace the first one, lost in a fire. The Albert County Historical Society runs the eight buildings as a museum, with the R. B. Bennett Commemorative Centre (opened 2010) for the prime minister who grew up here, and displays on Tom Collins, a convicted murderer.',
  'Eight original buildings: the 1845 gaol, tax office and records office, the 1904 courthouse, a carriage shed, a community hall and a library, with twenty-two themed galleries and a pair of cannons. Self-guided tours come with admission. Interior fittings not checked.',
  'A whole county seat of the 1840s, gaol and all, a few minutes from the Hopewell Rocks.',
  'Owned and run by the Albert County Historical Society at 3940 Route 114, Hopewell Cape. Admission in 2026: adult $10, senior or student $8, family $25 (its page). When read in November 2026 it was open by appointment and for groups until December 1, with tours from May 1 to November 30; regular summer hours were not on the page. Parking: a lot OpenStreetMap maps off Old Great Road beside the site.',
  [W('Albert County Museum'),L('Albert County Museum: hours and admission','https://albertcountymuseum.com/hours-admissions-index'),L('Albert County Museum: explore the site','https://www.albertcountymuseum.com/explore-theacm')],
  th=[45.84942,-64.57741],thn='Lot off Old Great Road (OpenStreetMap)',brick='?',hours='By appointment and for groups, May 1 to November 30; summer hours not given on its page'),
N('albert-mines-site','Albert Mines (albertite mine site)','Albert Mines','nb','industry','ruins',45.8853,-64.6898,'area','view','drive','','1840s–1881',
  'Abraham Gesner, the first government geologist in British North America, distilled kerosene from albertite, a shiny black bitumen found here, in 1846. He lost control of the mine in 1852 when a provincial court ruled albertite was coal; over the next 30 years the mine produced about 200,000 tons, much of it shipped to Boston and New York to make gas for street lamps (the Register; Wikipedia gives mining from 1854 to 1881). The province calls it the site of the first commercial extraction of petroleum products anywhere, and made it a Provincial Historic Site in 1998.',
  'A 324-hectare site of grass and woods along Old Albert Mines Road. The Register lists the ruins of mine shafts, tailings piles, the manager\'s house and a church; CBC (2022) adds remnants of homes and a school, and a main shaft deeper than the Empire State Building is tall. From the road it looks like any roadside lot.',
  'The black rock that gave the world kerosene, in a field south of Hillsborough.',
  'Private land: CBC News quotes the historian James Upham that the mine remnants are on private land and off limits to the public, and warns of the deep shaft. Look from Old Albert Mines Road only. No parking lot; the pin is the Register\'s point for the whole site, not a ruin.',
  [HP('Albert Mines Site',9423),W('Albert Mines, New Brunswick'),L('CBC News: Roadside history, Albert Mines','https://amp.cbc.ca/news/canada/new-brunswick/roadside-history-james-upham-albert-mines-oil-gas-extraction-1.6690858')],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('steeves-house-hillsborough','Steeves House Museum','Hillsborough','nb','landmark','standing',45.92489,-64.64562,'exact','open','walk','','1812–1840',
  'A two-storey wood-frame house that grew out of a one-room cottage between 1812 and 1840. It was the birthplace of William Henry Steeves, a delegate to the Charlottetown and Quebec conferences of 1864 and a Father of Confederation. From 1871 it was the house of the manager of the gypsum mill below it (the Albert Manufacturing Company, later Canadian Gypsum), and at least seventeen managers lived here. It is a Provincial Historic Site and a museum of the family and the area.',
  'Clapboard house with a big attached barn, a central door with sidelights, six-over-six windows and a bay of narrow windows on the east side looking down to the Petitcodiac. Inside, says the Register: four fireplaces, a curving central stair with a mahogany rail, original crown moulding and bright blue fireplace tiles in the dining room, a servants\' back stair with thick glass set in the treads, hidden shelves, and stone cisterns and tapered stone walls in the cellar.',
  'A Father of Confederation\'s birthplace that became the gypsum boss\'s house.',
  'A museum at 40 Mill Street, Hillsborough, off Route 114. Guided tours adult $10, senior or student $8; self-guided $5 (its page, 2026). Off season by appointment. Street parking on Mill Street (not checked).',
  [HP('Steeves House',13983),L('Steeves House Museum: visit','https://www.steeveshousemuseum.ca/visit')],brick='no',hours='June 18 to September 6, 2026: Wednesday to Monday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m. (closed Tuesday); off season by appointment'),
N('nb-railway-museum-hillsborough','New Brunswick Railway Museum','Hillsborough','nb','rail','standing',45.92316,-64.64484,'site','open','walk','stations','1877–1982',
  'The Salisbury and Albert Railway reached Hillsborough in 1877 and served the village for 105 years, a branch off the European and North American line between Saint John and Shediac; before it, horse-drawn tramways on wooden rails carried albertite and gypsum to the river from 1851. When Canadian National gave up the branch after the gypsum quarry closed, volunteers ran it as the Salem and Hillsborough tourist railroad from 1984. The station burned in 1994, and the present museum building was built to look like a rural station.',
  'A one-storey museum building in the style of a country station, with a wrap-around awning on big brackets, holding small railway artefacts; outside, track through the old station yard with diner, passenger, baggage and freight cars, heavy rail equipment, a steam engine and the original section shed with its track cars (the Register). The old gypsum tramway ran just south of it.',
  'Climb aboard the last steam engine of a branch line that ran on borrowed money.',
  'Owned and run by the New Brunswick Division of the Canadian Railroad Historical Association (Wikipedia) at 2847 Main Street. No page of its own was found, so opening times are not checked. OpenStreetMap maps several small lots nearby; the one beside it was not checked.',
  [HP('New Brunswick Railway Museum',18394),W('New Brunswick Railway Museum')],brick='no',hours='?'),
N('albert-manufacturing-office-hillsborough','Albert Manufacturing Company office and gypsum mill buildings','Hillsborough','nb','industry','standing',45.92281,-64.64528,'exact','view','walk','','1873–1980',
  'The Tompkins family of New York set up the Albert Manufacturing Company here in 1854 to quarry the escarpment\'s gypsum and mill it into Hammer Brand plaster, sold across North America; about 200 men worked the quarries and 100 the mill. The first mill burned in 1873 and the second in 1911, each replaced bigger. The company built this office in 1907, sold out to United States Gypsum in 1930, and the mill closed in 1980; the office is now the village office and library.',
  'A one-storey Edwardian Classical office of red brick with quoins, a hipped roof with copper finials, sandstone lintels and sills under segmental-arched windows, a tall brick chimney and a pedimented entrance on square columns. Beside it the Register lists two early mill buildings of 1873, among them a steep-roofed storage shed, and the twin concrete gypsum silos of 1932. Interior not checked.',
  'The office of the plaster that dried harder than any other, with its silos still standing.',
  'Village of Hillsborough offices and library at 2849 Main Street (the Register); look from the street. Whether the mill buildings and silos are on public ground was not checked. Opening hours of the office and library not checked.',
  [HP('Albert Manufacturing Co. Office',18370)],brick='yes'),
N('riverside-consolidated-school','Riverside Consolidated School','Riverside-Albert','nb','civic','standing',45.74826,-64.73134,'exact','view','drive','','1905',
  'A three-storey school of 1905, built when rural schools were being "consolidated" into larger ones with manual training, household science and school gardens, the cause of the Montreal tobacco millionaire Sir William Macdonald. Senator Abner Reid McClelan, later Lieutenant-Governor, gave a quarter of the cost on condition it teach those subjects. It was designed by Watson E. Reid of Harvey and built by Sackville contractors; the Register calls it the oldest consolidated school still in use in New Brunswick.',
  'Stained wood shingles with white trim and a band of vertical boards under a bracketed eave, on a foundation of red sandstone quarried at Midway and New Ireland nearby; tall slender classroom windows, a central gabled projection, one tall brick chimney, and separate entrances still labelled "Boys" and "Girls". Most original finishes survive (the Register). Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'A working school at 90 Water Street, beside a branch of the Shepody River: look from the street, and not during school hours.',
  [HP('Riverside Consolidated School',2207)],brick='no'),
N('victoria-manor-riverside-albert','Victoria Manor (McClelan house)','Riverside-Albert','nb','landmark','standing',45.7527,-64.7255,'site','view','drive','','1893',
  'Abner Reid McClelan, shipowner, merchant, senator and Lieutenant-Governor of New Brunswick from 1896 to 1902, built this Queen Anne Revival house in 1893 and spent his last years in it. Watson E. Reid, the Harvey-born architect who also drew the school down the road, designed it. It is a Provincial Historic Site.',
  'A two-and-a-half-storey house with a curved two-storey front tower under a conical roof, bays of several shapes, clapboard below and patterned shingles above, colonnaded verandahs and red sandstone walks and foundation. The Register says the interior is unaltered, with stained hardwood panelling, and a hand-painted stained-glass window of Queen Victoria in her Diamond Jubilee regalia. Whether anyone may go in was not checked.',
  '',
  'On Maple Street in Riverside-Albert. Who owns it now and whether it opens was not checked: look from the street. The pin is the Register\'s point for the house.',
  [HP('Victoria Manor',3205)],brick='no'),
N('coverdale-united-church','Coverdale United Church','Coverdale','nb','worship','standing',46.04581,-64.86559,'exact','view','drive','','1865',
  'Methodists built a plain 24-by-32-foot meeting house here in 1828, the only Methodist church on the preaching circuit between Dorchester and Saint John. A generation later, in 1865, they built this larger church across the road, one of the first in the district with Gothic detail; the congregation joined the United Church of Canada in 1925. It is a Provincial Historic Site.',
  'A white clapboard Carpenter Gothic church facing north over the Petitcodiac, with lancet windows, a Gothic door surround and a square tower carrying an eight-sided upper stage with pinnacles and battlements. Inside, says the Register, the 1865 plan survives: a rectangular room with a small gallery facing the pulpit, renovated several times in the 20th century. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'On Route 112 at 1174 Coverdale Road, where the Turtle Creek Road meets it, outside Riverview. No service times or opening were found: look from the road.',
  [HP('Coverdale United Church',5734)],brick='no'),
# ---- The Fundy shore ----
N('cape-enrage-lighthouse','Cape Enrage Lighthouse','Cape Enrage','nb','light','standing',45.59392,-64.77992,'exact','open','walk','','1840',
  'The first lighthouse in the upper Bay of Fundy was lit here in the spring of 1840, a square tower in which the keeper\'s family lived. It was replaced by a new tower lit in 1870, and the one standing now was built in 1904 (Lighthouse Friends). The light was automated in 1988 and the station left empty; by 1993 every building but the tower was to be pulled down, until a Moncton physics teacher, Dennison Tate, and his high-school students began restoring it and running it as a summer business.',
  'The 1904 tower: square, with sloping sides and a red octagonal lantern, 29 feet tall and 125 feet above the water (Lighthouse Friends), on the cliff top with the keeper\'s house of 1952 (Wikipedia) and the other station buildings. Interior not checked.',
  'The student-saved light on the cliffs above the Fundy tides.',
  'Who owns the tower differs by source: Wikipedia says the Coast Guard transferred it in 2004 to Cape Enrage Adventures and the Cape Enrage Interpretive Centre (unsourced there), while Lighthouse Friends says the Coast Guard owns it; the keeper\'s house went to the Province in 1995 (Wikipedia). Lighthouse Friends says the cape is open all year and the centre\'s services (and a restaurant) run from the Victoria Day weekend to mid-October. The operator\'s own site (capeenrage.ca) answered 403 here, so hours and fees are not checked. Parking at the lot on Cape Enrage Road just north of the light.',
  [W('Cape Enrage Lighthouse'),L('Lighthouse Friends: Cape Enrage','https://lighthousefriends.com/light.asp?ID=1379')],th=[45.59452,-64.77964],thn='Cape Enrage Road lot (OpenStreetMap)',conf='mixed',brick='no',hours='?'),
N('fundy-park-headquarters-alma','Fundy National Park headquarters (Visitor Information Centre East)','Alma','nb','inst','standing',45.5955,-64.9509,'site','open','walk','','1948–1949',
  'Built in 1948-49 as the administration building of Fundy National Park, New Brunswick\'s first national park, above the mouth of the Upper Salmon River at Alma. Its English-cottage design in fieldstone set the style for the park\'s later buildings. It is still the park\'s control centre and its eastern visitor centre, and a Recognized Federal Heritage Building.',
  'A one-and-a-half-storey fieldstone building with a steep gabled roof, a stone chimney, dormers of different sizes, a porch made by carrying the roof over the door, original six-over-six windows and a large multi-pane window, on open lawns (the Register). Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Parks Canada, at the Alma entrance of Fundy National Park on Route 114. A park entry fee applies (Parks Canada). Parking: lots OpenStreetMap maps beside the building on Route 114.',
  [HP('Visitor Information Centre East (Administration Building)',10096),FUNDYH],th=[45.59569,-64.95168],thn='Visitor centre lot on Route 114 (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no',hours='May 15 to October 12: daily 8 a.m. to 7:45 p.m.'),
N('fundy-saltwater-pool','Fundy saltwater pool and bathhouse','Alma','nb','inst','standing',45.5906,-64.9527,'site','open','walk','','1950',
  'One of the first things built in Fundy National Park, in 1950: a concrete pool filled with Bay of Fundy water by a pump worked by the tides, beside a stone bathhouse by the Saint John architect Herbert Stanley Brenan. The Register calls it a very late example of the Rustic style of the national parks, and among the last of the swimming pools the parks built before their 1964 policy turned away from such recreation.',
  'A single-storey bathhouse of rough-cut, irregular stone under a cedar-shingled roof, with an arched portico toward the pool, small deep-set windows under the eaves and tripartite windows in the gables; the pool, now heated (Parks Canada), along its north side.',
  'Swim in a pool the tides fill.',
  'Parks Canada, off the Point Wolfe Road in Fundy National Park. Pool fees apply, and passes are sold at the pool house; lifeguards on duty, last admission 5:30 p.m. (Parks Canada). Parking: a lot on the Point Wolfe Road beside it (OpenStreetMap).',
  [HP('Saltwater Pool and Bathhouse',13090),L('Parks Canada: Fundy swimming','https://parks.canada.ca/pn-np/nb/fundy/activ/natation-swimming'),FUNDYH],th=[45.59032,-64.95276],thn='Point Wolfe Road lot (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no',hours='Late June to early September (June 19 to September 7 on the hours page): daily 11 a.m. to 5:45 p.m.'),
# ---- Dorchester ----
N('keillor-house-dorchester','Keillor House','Dorchester','nb','landmark','standing',45.90079,-64.51643,'exact','open','walk','','1813',
  'John Keillor, a farmer, justice of the peace and later a judge of the county court, built this stone house in 1813. Two single-storey wings flank a two-storey central block in the Palladian manner, which the Register calls rare for a stone house in the Atlantic region. It is a Provincial Heritage Place and the Westmorland Historical Society\'s museum.',
  'Coursed ashlar on the front and chimneys and rougher squared stone elsewhere; the central block has bevelled rusticated joints and flat-arched windows on the ground floor, rounded belt courses, a gambrel-like hipped roof, and wings with roofs that do not match. Interior not checked.',
  'Dorchester\'s 1813 stone house with a wing on each side.',
  'A museum of the Westmorland Historical Society at 4974 Main Street, Dorchester; one paid admission covers it and the St. James Textile Museum across the street. The hours read are those the society gave for 2025; 2026 hours were not on the page. No lot is mapped: street parking on Main Street (not checked).',
  [HP('Keillor House',2353),KEIL],brick='no',hours='June 14 to August 31 (2025 season): Wednesday to Sunday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.'),
N('st-james-textile-museum-dorchester','Saint James Presbyterian Church (St. James Textile Museum)','Dorchester','nb','worship','standing',45.90123,-64.51750,'exact','open','walk','','1884',
  'A Carpenter Gothic Presbyterian church built in 1884. It was given to the Westmorland Historical Society in 1970 and opened as a museum in 1984, holding the area\'s textile history with blacksmith\'s tools and a working foot lathe among the collection (the Register).',
  'A white wood-frame church with a big square corner tower and pyramidal roof holding the entrance, a steep front gable, decorative bargeboards, hood mouldings and pointed Gothic windows with tracery and stained glass (the Register). Inside, the museum.',
  '',
  'Westmorland Historical Society, 4967 Main Street, Dorchester, across from Keillor House; admission is shared with it. Hours as given for 2025; 2026 hours not on the page.',
  [HP('Saint James Presbyterian Church',17461),KEIL],brick='no',hours='June 14 to August 31 (2025 season): Wednesday to Sunday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.'),
N('bell-inn-dorchester','Bell Inn','Dorchester','nb','hotel','standing',45.89890,-64.51674,'exact','view','walk','','1811–1821',
  'A one-storey stone house built some time between 1811 and 1821, the Register says one of the oldest surviving stone buildings in New Brunswick, probably of stone from the quarried outcrop behind it. It was a small inn for decades before William Hickman, later a leading Dorchester shipbuilder, bought it in 1858 and ran it with his stagecoach line between Moncton and Amherst; it was Dorchester\'s only stagecoach inn until the Intercolonial Railway came in 1872. Hickman then made it his house and office, and his family kept it until 1948.',
  'Coursed ashlar on the front, squared stone quoins and rougher stone on the gables, five bays with a central door, tall cut-stone chimneys at both ends, clapboarded dormers, and original windows throughout (6-over-6, 10-over-10, 9-over-9); a wooden side ell with a storefront at its low ground floor. Inside, says the Register, the original railing, Greek Revival trim and fireplaces. The stone outcrop is behind it. What it is used for now was not checked.',
  'The stagecoach inn cut from the rock behind it.',
  'At 3515 Cape Road, at the corner of the main road in Dorchester. Its present use and opening were not checked: look from the street.',
  [HP('Bell Inn',5749)],brick='no'),
N('rocklyn-chandler-house-dorchester','Rocklyn (Chandler House)','Dorchester','nb','landmark','standing',45.89873,-64.51505,'exact','view','walk','','1831',
  'Edward Barron Chandler, a Father of Confederation, had this Classical Revival house built in 1831 and owned it throughout his long public career; it is a National Historic Site.',
  'A two-storey, five-bay house of worked stone, rusticated on the ground floor and smooth ashlar above, under a low hipped slate roof between tall stone chimneys, with a wooden portico of fluted columns and triglyphs whose pediment repeats the roof\'s pitch, and a door with sidelights and fanlight. The Register lists interior shutters, cupboards and original mantels. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'At 5000 Main Street, Dorchester, in its own grounds. Ownership and any opening not checked: look from the street.',
  [HP('Chandler House / Rocklyn National Historic Site of Canada',12982)],brick='no'),
N('dorchester-penitentiary','Dorchester Penitentiary','Dorchester','nb','inst','standing',45.9114,-64.5105,'site','view','drive','','1880',
  'A federal penitentiary opened on July 14, 1880, on a hill above the Memramcook valley, and from the Second World War the maximum-security prison for all of Atlantic Canada. It is now a medium-security institution and, with Kingston closed in 2013, the second-oldest federal prison still working in Canada (Wikipedia). Its four stone corner towers of 1909-16 are Recognized Federal Heritage Buildings.',
  'From outside: the wall and its four guard towers, built like classical columns, a flared concrete base, square or octagonal stone shafts and a bracketed platform carrying an enclosed guard hut, with smooth quoins and stone voussoirs (the Register). The administration building is listed too.',
  'A working Victorian prison whose towers are built like columns.',
  'A working federal prison of the Correctional Service of Canada. No visiting: look from the public road only, and do not stop at the gates.',
  [W('Dorchester Penitentiary'),HP('Dorchester Penitentiary, Towers D-1 to D-4',4816)],brick='no'),
# ---- Sackville ----
N('campbell-carriage-factory','Campbell Carriage Factory','Sackville','nb','industry','standing',45.92698,-64.35208,'exact','open','walk','','1838–1951',
  'Built in 1838 as a tannery on the edge of the Tantramar marshes at Middle Sackville, it was bought in 1855 by Ronald Campbell and made into a carriage factory, which turned out carriages, sleighs, tools, farm equipment and coffins. The Register gives 1949 for the closing, the Trust 1951 for the last wages; the last two workers locked it with everything inside. The Tantramar Heritage Trust restored it from 1998 around the 6,000 objects it found, and it is a Provincial Historic Site.',
  'The two-storey post-and-beam factory, 21 by 9 metres, with its woodworking and machinery rooms below and storage, assembly and paint rooms above, still holding original tools, patterns, machinery and work in progress (the Register); a blacksmith shop, a warehouse, a carriage shed with carriages and sleighs made here, and a monument to the Acadian village of Tintamarre.',
  'A carriage works left locked with the work still on the benches.',
  'Run by the Tantramar Heritage Trust at 19 Church Street, Middle Sackville (Route 940 north from Exit 504, right at Silver Lake). Admission by donation; the ground floor, the forge and the warehouse are wheelchair accessible (its page). When read in November 2026 it was closed for the season, with tours by arrangement; summer hours were not on the page. Street parking on Church Street (not checked).',
  [HP('Campbell Carriage Factory',2254),L('Tantramar Heritage Trust: Campbell Carriage Factory Museum','https://tantramarheritage.ca/museums/campbell-carriage-factory/')],conf='mixed',brick='no',hours='Closed for the season in November 2026, tours by arrangement; summer hours not given on its page'),
N('boultenhouse-heritage-centre','Boultenhouse Heritage Centre','Sackville','nb','landmark','standing',45.89133,-64.37096,'exact','open','walk','','1789–1840s',
  'Two houses joined into one on a hill above the old shipyard by Captain\'s Corner. At the back is George Bulmer\'s house, a one-and-a-half-storey Georgian house of two rooms round a central chimney, dated to 1789-91 by Mount Allison University\'s tree-ring lab; in front is the Greek Revival house the shipbuilder Christopher Boultenhouse built about 1840. Captain Stephen Atkinson is also tied to it. The Tantramar Heritage Trust runs it.',
  'The shingled Bulmer house with its attic windows and central chimney; the Boultenhouse front with a Palladian window, a double door with transom and sidelights, panelled pilasters, a pedimented gable with a small fanlight, and a cut-stone basement, looking over the Tantramar River wharves. Interior not checked.',
  'A shipbuilder\'s house built onto a cottage of 1790.',
  'Tantramar Heritage Trust, 29 Queens Road, Sackville. Hours as the Trust\'s front page gave them in November 2026; tours outside them by arrangement. Parking: a lot OpenStreetMap maps on Queens Road (not checked).',
  [HP('Boultenhouse Heritage Centre',5306),THT],brick='no',hours='Tuesday to Friday 10 a.m. to 2 p.m.'),
N('sackville-railway-station','Sackville railway station (Intercolonial Railway)','Sackville','nb','rail','standing',45.89019,-64.36749,'exact','view','walk','stations','1907–1908',
  'The Intercolonial Railway built this sandstone station in 1907-08 at the foot of the hill by the Tantramar marshes, in its short prosperous spell before the Canadian National took it over. The Register calls it one of only three stone stations left in New Brunswick; it is a federal Heritage Railway Station (1994).',
  'A long, low station of rock-faced local sandstone under a high bell-cast hipped roof, with deep eaves on wooden brackets set on carved stone corbels, a small round-arched dormer over each bay, and a date stone in the operator\'s bay. The Register lists original trim and a panelled ceiling in the baggage room, traces of the old timber platform, and a wooden freight shed by the tracks. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'At the south end of Lorne Street. Whether trains still stop and when it opens was not checked: look from the street, and keep off the tracks of the working main line. The pin is the Register\'s point.',
  [HP('Canadian National Railways (Intercolonial Railway) Station',4592)],brick='no'),
N('sackville-harness-shop','Sackville Harness Shop','Sackville','nb','civic','standing',45.89504,-64.37036,'site','view','walk','','c. 1846',
  'Samuel Freeze Black built a store here on Main Street about 1846; the Register calls it one of the first commercial buildings in the area. Sackville Harness, set up in 1919, made harness and handmade horse collars here, one of the last makers in Canada. CBC reported in April 2021 that the owner was closing the shop that May for want of a buyer and selling the stock and the building separately; CBC called it the town\'s oldest building.',
  'A two-storey shingled shop with a steep front gable, a painted decorative cornice, big storefront windows either side of the door, painted corner boards and a painted wall sign on the side toward downtown (the Register). What it is now was not checked.',
  '',
  'At 39 Main Street. The harness business closed in 2021 (CBC); the building\'s present owner and use were not checked: look from the street.',
  [HP('Sackville Harness Shop',5264),L('CBC News: Sackville Harness shop closes (2021)','https://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/new-brunswick/sackville-harness-shop-horses-leather-1.5979365')],brick='no'),
]
